    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Henry Buck moved from Maine in the 1820s to establish the lumber mills that gave Bucksport its name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bucksport%2C_South_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Sawmills in Bucksport and nearby Bucksville produced three million board feet of lumber annually by 1850.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bucksport%2C_South_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lumber from the Bucksport operation was even used in the construction of the Brooklyn Bridge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bucksport%2C_South_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Bucksport ships once transported local lumber to major cities like New York and Boston, and even to other countries.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
